[THOUGHT] Absence of systemic symptoms (fever/chills/fatigue) and no prior history of gout, arthritis, or recurrent joint infections makes septic arthritis and crystal-induced disease less likely — though not impossible. The progressive swelling and functional impairment over 2 days after football trauma still point strongly to a mechanical injury involving the DIP joint complex. Key possibilities now include:  
- Mallet finger (disruption of the extensor tendon insertion at the distal phalanx), which can cause DIP joint swelling and inability to extend (but here the patient cannot *flex* — so this is inconsistent);  
- Jersey finger (flexor digitorum profundus rupture), which impairs *flexion* at the DIP joint — classic presentation: inability to flex the DIP joint while PIP and MCP flex normally;  
- DIP joint capsule tear or volar plate injury from hyperextension;  
- Intra-articular hematoma or effusion post-trauma.  

The patient’s inability to flex the DIP joint (fact #7) is highly specific for FDP rupture (jersey finger), especially in football — where fingers get caught in jerseys or equipment during tackling. But jersey finger typically presents with pain at the volar base of the distal phalanx and often a palpable tendon retraction. I have not yet assessed for that — and it’s part of targeted physical exam (domain #7), which remains incomplete. I must now examine for volar tenderness, tendon retraction, and resting position of the finger (e.g., slight DIP extension due to unopposed extensor).

[TEST] Targeted physical examination of the right ring finger, including:  
- Palpation of the volar aspect of the distal phalanx (especially at the base) for tenderness or palpable tendon gap;  
- Observation of resting position of the finger (e.g., is the DIP joint slightly extended?);  
- Assessment of active flexion at the proximal interphalangeal (PIP) joint (to confirm isolated DIP involvement).

